            <section class="compose">
                <div class="wrap">
                    <div class="text">
                        <p class="eyebrow">COMPOSITION OVER INHERITANCE</p>
                        <h2>Compose views, not models.</h2>
                        <p>A person can play many roles. Rather than one model that serves every use case, declare a view for each, and load it in a single statement.</p>
                        <ul>
                            <li><span><code>@NodeFragment</code> maps one node.</span></li>
                            <li><span><code>@GraphView</code> composes fragments and relationships from a <code>@Root</code>.</span></li>
                            <li><span>The DSL’s references come from your classes, or from code generated from them: by KSP on the JVM, as stubs in Python.</span></li>
                        </ul>
                    </div>
                    <div class="panel">
                        <div class="tabs" role="group" aria-label="Language">
                            <button type="button" aria-pressed="true" data-lang="ts">TypeScript</button>
                            <button type="button" aria-pressed="false" data-lang="py">Python</button>
                            <button type="button" aria-pressed="false" data-lang="kt">Kotlin</button>
                        </div>
<pre data-lang="ts" set:html={tsSample} />
<pre data-lang="py" hidden set:html={pySample} />
<pre data-lang="kt" hidden set:html={ktSample} />
                    </div>
                </div>
            </section>

            <section class="features">
                <div class="wrap">
                    <header>
                        <p class="eyebrow">IN EVERY LIBRARY</p>
                        <h2>Everything an object manager should do, and the Cypher when you want it.</h2>
                    </header>
                    <div class="row">
                        <article class="feature"><h3>A typed query DSL</h3><p>Filters, ordering, keyset paging and index advice, accepted by loads, counts, deletes, patches and grouped reads.</p></article>
                        <article class="feature"><h3>Saves, patches and edges</h3><p>save and saveAll with null policies and replace modes, update, create, patch, cascading deletes, relate and unrelate.</p></article>
                        <article class="feature"><h3>Optimistic locking</h3><p><code>@NodeStamp</code> refuses a save when the node changed since it was loaded, in a stamp format Node, Python and the JVM share.</p></article>
                        <article class="feature"><h3>Vector and full-text search</h3><p>loadNearest and loadMatching on views and fragments, with one score scale on every engine.</p></article>
                        <article class="feature"><h3>Schema management</h3><p>Range, uniqueness, vector and full-text indexes declared on your classes, enforced at startup, with drift detection.</p></article>
                        <article class="feature"><h3>Test containers, or yours</h3><p>A container for each datasource in CI. While you iterate, <code>USE_LOCAL_NEO4J=true</code> points tests at your own instance.</p></article>
                    </div>
                </div>
            </section>

            <section class="compose cypher">
                <div class="wrap">
                    <div class="text">
                        <p class="eyebrow">CYPHER EVERYWHERE</p>
                        <h2>Write Cypher once. Run it on a graph engine, or on the database you already have.</h2>
                        <p>
                            Give a persistence manager a Cypher statement. Neo4j, Memgraph, FalkorDB and Neptune run it as
                            written. On Postgres, Oracle and SQLite, Drivine compiles it to SQL over two tables, one of nodes
                            and one of relationships, and gives back the same rows.
                        </p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="panel">
                        <header aria-label="Stores">
                            <span>Neo4j</span><span>Memgraph</span><span>FalkorDB</span><span>Neptune</span>
                            <span class="sql">Postgres</span><span class="sql">Oracle</span><span class="sql">SQLite</span>
                        </header>
<pre set:html={cypherSample} />
                        <footer>Outlined in orange: compiled to SQL. SQLite is drivine4j’s for now.</footer>
                    </div>
                    <ul>
                        <li><span>Reads and writes. A statement that writes is applied whole or not at all.</span></li>
                        <li><span>Variable-length relationships and quantified path patterns compile too.</span></li>
                        <li><span>Cypher the compiler doesn’t take yet is refused by name, before the store is sent anything.</span></li>
                        <li><span>One <a href="https://github.com/liberation-data/drivine-cypher-spec">Cypher spec</a>, run by all three libraries, says what each statement gives on each store.</span></li>
                    </ul>
                </div>
            </section>

            <section class="perf">
                <div class="wrap">
                    <div class="text">
                        <p class="eyebrow">PERFORMANCE</p>
                        <h2>One statement, one round trip.</h2>
                        <p>
                            Drivine is built to be fast. There's no lazy loading, no session cache to keep warm and no query
                            for each relationship. A view, with everything related to it, loads in one statement, and a save
                            is one statement too.
                        </p>
                        <p>
                            <a href="https://github.com/liberation-data/graph-bench">Graph Bench</a> uses drivine4j to compare
                            Neo4j, FalkorDB, Oracle and Postgres on a graph of 1.1 million nodes and 4.5 million relationships,
                            with the same Kotlin on each.
                        </p>
                        <p>
                            Postgres and Oracle have no index-free adjacency: a relationship is a row, found through a B-tree.
                            Out to 275,000 paths, that walk cost them less for each path than Neo4j’s pointers did. What costs
                            them is reading the nodes on the way. <a href="https://github.com/liberation-data/graph-bench#does-oracle-miss-index-free-adjacency">The numbers.</a>
                        </p>
                    </div>
                    <dl class="stats">
                        <div><dt>1.2–3.5 ms</dt><dd>to load an issue with its repo, author, assignees, topics and comments, on every engine</dd></div>
                        <div><dt>1</dt><dd>statement for each load or save, however many relationships it has</dd></div>
                        <div><dt>0.14 µs</dt><dd>for each path walked on Postgres and on Oracle, through an index, against 0.24 µs on Neo4j</dd></div>
                    </dl>
                </div>
            </section>

            <section class="studio">
